---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "Best Weight Plates (2026): Iron and Rubber-Coated Picks";
const description =
  "Five weight plate picks checked against makers and reviews: iron vs coated, Olympic vs standard, tolerance, grip handles, sets vs pairs and price per pound.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "rep-equalizer-urethane-plates",
    name: "REP Urethane Coated Equalizer Plates (45 lb Pair)",
    brand: "REP",
    category: "Urethane-coated cast-iron grip plates",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B07VZ1Z9RS?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41fk1d-ZFNL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$26–$280 per pair",
    priceBounds: {"low":26,"high":280},
    specs: "Pairs 2.5–45 lb · sets 175–425 lb · urethane-coated cast iron · six grip holes · 450 mm (17.7 in.) diameter · 3% tolerance (maker) · lifetime warranty on 10 lb and up",
    pros: [
      "Urethane resists wear and, per REP and Garage Gym Reviews, has little or no odour",
      "Six symmetrical grip holes make plates easy to carry, load and use on their own",
      "Quieter against the bar, the rack and other plates than bare iron",
      "Lifetime warranty on the 10 lb and heavier plates, five years on the 2.5s and 5s",
    ],
    cons: [
      "About $3.45 a pound for a 245 lb set built from pairs, the most on this page",
      "A 3% tolerance, which Garage Gym Reviews calls below average",
      "Still iron plates: REP and reviewers both say not to drop them",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The plates to buy if you want quiet, odour-free, long-lasting plates for a gym inside the house and can pay for them.",
    description: "REP's urethane-coated Equalizers are Garage Gym Reviews' best weight plates overall, chosen for a durable coating and a thoughtful design. REP describes cast-iron plates encased in a commercial-grade, odour-free urethane, with six precision-cut grip holes, a debossed logo and white-inlaid weight markings, a 3% weight tolerance and a fit for any 2-inch Olympic bar. Garage Gym Reviews gives the same 450 mm (17.7-inch) diameter, the same weight range and a lifetime warranty, praises the low odour and reduced clang, and notes the tolerance is looser than average although its plates proved accurate. REP's warranty is five years on the 2.5 and 5 lb plates and lifetime on the 10 lb and heavier plates. On 19 October 2026 REP sold pairs from $25.99 for 2.5s to $279.99 for 45s, and six sets from 175 to 425 lb. Built from pairs, a 245 lb set comes to $845.94, about $3.45 a pound, against $2.16 for REP's plain iron. That premium buys a quieter, cleaner and longer-lasting plate, not one you can drop. On 9 November 2026 REP's own Amazon listings, titled \"REP Urethane Coated Plate v3\", described the same Equalizer plates — 17.7-inch diameter, six grip holes, CPU urethane over cast iron and a 3% tolerance — at the same $25.99 to $279.99 per pair; the button goes to the 45 lb pair, and the other sizes are listed separately.",
  },
  {
    id: "titan-cast-iron-olympic-plate",
    name: "Titan Fitness 45 LB Cast Iron Olympic Plate",
    brand: "Titan Fitness",
    category: "Classic cast-iron plates sold on Amazon",
    badge: "Best Value on Amazon",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B07K8XWFBP?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/410NIbgcZvL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $85 per 45 lb single (Amazon and Titan, 9 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":85,"high":85},
    specs: "Pairs 2.5–25 lb, singles 35 and 45 lb, 245 lb set (Titan's store) · cast iron, silver hammered finish · raised lip · 50 mm collar opening (Titan) · 1-year warranty · no published tolerance",
    pros: [
      "About $1.89 a pound in 45s, the same as REP's Old School 45s",
      "Sold on Amazon as well as on Titan's store",
      "A raised lip makes the plates easier to pick up off the floor",
      "Thin, handle-free plates leave room for more weight on the bar",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Titan publishes no weight tolerance; weigh and pair the plates when they arrive",
      "Titan's store showed every size except the 45 lb single out of stock on 9 November 2026",
      "Noisy, like all bare iron, and only a 1-year warranty",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The bare cast-iron plate to buy on Amazon for a rack-based strength gym on a budget; REP's Old School plates are the equivalent on REP's own store.",
    description: "REP's Old School iron plates, the best-value plates in this guide, are sold on REP's own store and we could not match them to an Amazon US listing, so this card shows the closest classic cast-iron plate that Amazon does sell. Titan's Cast Iron Olympic Plates are bare, single-sided iron plates in a silver hammered finish with a raised lip for pickup. Titan's own page, checked on 9 November 2026, lists 2.5, 5, 10 and 25 lb pairs, 35 and 45 lb singles and a 245 lb set, a 50 mm collar opening and a one-year warranty; Titan's page for the 45 lb single gives 50.6 mm, so expect a standard Olympic fit rather than either exact figure. Titan publishes no weight tolerance, so weigh and pair the plates when they arrive. That day Titan's store showed the 45 lb single at $84.97, reduced from $104.99, and Amazon listed the same 45 lb single at $84.99, about $1.89 a pound, while the smaller sizes and the 245 lb set ($549.99) were out of stock at Titan. Like all bare iron they are loud and should not be dropped, but for squats, presses, rows and deadlifts lowered under control in a rack they do the job for about what REP's Old School plates cost.",
  },
  {
    id: "rep-rubber-coated-olympic-plates",
    name: "REP Rubber Coated Olympic Plates (45 lb Pair)",
    brand: "REP",
    category: "Rubber-coated iron plates with stainless steel collars",
    badge: "Coated Iron on Amazon",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B07N31RLPM?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41knkiYW47L._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$24–$220 per pair (Amazon, 9 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":24,"high":220},
    specs: "Pairs 2.5–45 lb · rubber-coated cast iron · 2 in. stainless steel collars · built-in handles · 159–444 mm diameter · 3% tolerance (maker) · 1-year warranty",
    pros: [
      "Stainless steel collars in the centre hole, which protect the bar sleeve and resist rust",
      "Rubber coating that REP says protects the floor and the bar and cuts noise",
      "Built-in handles for carrying, loading and plate exercises",
      "Sold on Amazon by REP in pairs from 2.5 to 45 lb",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Not made in the USA, unlike REP’s Equalizer iron plates",
      "A 3% tolerance and a 1-year warranty, by REP’s figures",
      "Still iron plates: REP says not to drop them",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The coated iron plate to buy on Amazon if you want handles, stainless collars and a quieter plate than bare iron for less than urethane.",
    description: "REP's US-made Equalizer iron plates are sold on REP's own store and we could not find them on Amazon, so this card shows the REP iron plate that is sold there: its rubber-coated Olympic plates. REP's Amazon listings and a Newegg listing agree on cast-iron plates coated in rubber, with 2-inch stainless steel collars in the centre hole and diameters from 159 to 444 mm; REP's product page and FAQ give a 3% weight tolerance and a one-year warranty, and say the plates are not bumper plates and should not be dropped, because that can damage the bar, the plates and the floor. The Amazon listings describe low-odour rubber, embossed red markings and built-in handles. On 9 November 2026 REP's Amazon pairs ran from $23.99 for 2.5s to $219.99 for 45s; the button goes to the 45 lb pair. Against the Equalizer iron plates this card replaces, you give up US manufacture, the machine-drilled hole and the 2% tolerance; you gain a coating that is quieter on the rack and kinder to floors, and stainless collars that make loading smoother. We found no independent review of these plates specifically, so the figures are REP's and its retailers'.",
  },
  {
    id: "cap-olympic-grip-plates",
    name: "CAP Barbell Olympic Cast Iron Grip Plates",
    brand: "CAP Barbell",
    category: "Budget cast-iron grip plates, singles and packs",
    badge: "Lowest Price",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B01B262O54?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51ijp8MqYqL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$115–$360 per pack",
    priceBounds: {"low":115,"high":360},
    specs: "2.5 lb (six), 5 lb (four), 10 lb (four), 25, 35 and 45 lb singles · packs 75–285 lb · cast iron, semi-gloss finish · grip cut-outs · 2 in. centre hole",
    pros: [
      "About $1.26 a pound in CAP's 285 lb pack, the cheapest per pound here",
      "Sold on Amazon, according to both BarBend and Garage Gym Reviews",
      "Grip cut-outs make loading and carrying easier",
      "Heavy plates sold as singles, so you can add one at a time",
    ],
    cons: [
      "CAP publishes no weight tolerance",
      "Garage Gym Reviews says CAP's coating chips and scuffs easily",
      "BarBend reports only a 30-day warranty",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The cheapest reputable way to load a bar. Weigh the plates when they arrive and pair them up.",
    description: "CAP's grip plates are the budget plates BarBend recommends for buyers on a tight budget, and the plates Garage Gym Reviews calls the best bet if you want very cheap plates from Amazon. CAP describes solid cast-iron plates with oversized grip cut-outs, a semi-gloss finish and weights picked out in silver, for any 2-inch Olympic bar or machine. CAP's store and BarBend list the same bundles: 2.5 lb plates in sixes, 5 and 10 lb plates in fours, and 25, 35 and 45 lb plates as singles. On 19 October 2026 CAP sold singles from $29.99 for six 2.5s to $59.99 for a 45, and plate packs from 75 lb ($114.99) to 285 lb ($359.99), or about $1.26 a pound. BarBend lists Amazon prices of $20.99 to $62.99 for the individual items, and reports a 30-day manufacturer warranty from the date of sale. The savings come with trade-offs: CAP publishes no tolerance, there are no kilogram versions, and Garage Gym Reviews warns that the finish chips and scuffs easily, so expect cosmetic wear. For a first set of plates that will be loaded and unloaded in a rack, they are hard to beat on price.",
  },
  {
    id: "titan-grip-plates-pair",
    name: "Titan Fitness Olympic Grip Weight Plates (5 lb Pair)",
    brand: "Titan Fitness",
    category: "Rubber-coated cast-iron grip plates, sold on Amazon by the pair",
    badge: "Rubber-Coated on Amazon",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09JQ4TPCB?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41NHQ0bcGYL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$35.99 per 5 lb pair (Amazon, 9 Nov 2026); $114.99 per 45 lb single at Titan",
    priceBounds: {"low":35.99,"high":114.99},
    specs: "Rubber-coated cast iron · four contoured grip handles · 450 mm diameter and 50.6 mm collar opening on the 45 lb plate · 1-year warranty (Titan's figures; not independently confirmed)",
    pros: [
      "Titan's rubber-coated grip plates, listed on Amazon by Titan",
      "Rubber coating protects floors and racks and reduces clang",
      "Contoured grip handles on every plate",
      "Heavier plates, up to 55 lb singles, and a 245 lb set are sold on Titan's store",
    ],
    cons: [
      "We found only the 5 lb pair on Amazon; the 245 lb set and heavier plates are on Titan's store",
      "Carded on Titan's figures only; we found no independent review",
      "Rubber can smell when new, and only a 1-year warranty",
    ],
    bottomLine: "Titan's rubber-coated grip plates are a sensible middle ground between bare iron and urethane; Amazon has the small pairs, Titan's own store the full 245 lb set.",
    description: "Titan's black rubber-coated grip plates were carded here as a 245 lb set, which is sold on Titan's own store; we could not find the set on Amazon US, so this card links the same plate line where Amazon does list it, as Titan's 5 lb pair. On 9 November 2026 Titan's page for its grip plates described a cast-iron core with a matte black rubber coating and four contoured grip handles, sold in pairs of 2.5, 5, 10 and 25 lb, singles of 35, 45 and 55 lb and a complete 245 lb set, and gave the 45 lb plate a 450 mm diameter, a 50.6 mm collar opening, a $114.99 price and a one-year warranty. Amazon listed Titan's 5 lb pair, described as Olympic grip plates in rubber-coated cast iron, at $35.99 the same day. Those are Titan's figures only: we found no independent review of these plates, so weigh them when they arrive. They suit a buyer adding change plates to an existing set, or anyone who wants quieter plates with handles at well under urethane prices.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Home Gym", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/home-gym/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Weight Plates", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-weight-plates/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What are the best weight plates for a home gym?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most people, Olympic 2-inch plates with grip handles from a maker that publishes a tolerance. REP's urethane-coated Equalizers are the best overall if you can pay about $3.45 a pound for a set; REP's Old School iron plates are the best value at about $2.16 on REP's own store, and Titan's cast-iron plates cost about the same in 45s on Amazon; CAP's grip plates are the cheapest at about $1.26.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are rubber-coated plates better than cast iron?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They are quieter and kinder to floors and racks, and less likely to chip, but they cost more and can smell when new. Cast iron is cheaper, thinner and lasts indefinitely. Neither should be dropped. Choose coated plates for a gym in the house or a shared space, and iron for a garage on a budget.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between Olympic and standard weight plates?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Olympic plates have a centre hole of about 2 inches and fit Olympic bars with 50 mm rotating sleeves. Standard plates have a 1-inch hole and fit 1-inch bars and spinlock handles. They are not interchangeable. For a new home gym, buy Olympic plates, because racks, bars and plate-loaded machines are built around them.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you drop rubber-coated weight plates?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Rubber- and urethane-coated plates are still iron plates, and REP says it does not recommend dropping any iron plate because it can damage the bar, the plates and the floor. If you plan to drop the bar in cleans, snatches or heavy deadlifts, buy bumper plates, which are made for it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much should weight plates cost per pound?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On 19 October 2026, the plates we checked ranged from about $1.26 a pound for CAP's cast-iron grip plates in a large pack to about $3.45 for REP's urethane-coated plates in a 245 lb set. Small plates cost much more per pound than 45s, and shipping can change the comparison.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What weight plates should a beginner buy?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A 245 lb set — a pair each of 45, 35, 25, 10, 5 and 2.5 lb plates — loads a 45 lb bar up to 290 lb and covers most beginners for a long time; a second pair of 10s fills the gaps between 5 lb steps. A 175 lb set without the 35s is a cheaper start; add a second pair of 45s when your deadlift needs it.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-19",
      dateModified: "2026-10-19",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 5,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-weight-plates/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/best-weight-plates/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "home-gym");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const MORE = (GROUP?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-19",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-19",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best weight plates",
      "olympic weight plates",
      "cast iron weight plates",
      "rubber coated weight plates",
      "weight plate set",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Best Overall: REP Urethane Coated Equalizer Plates (45 lb Pair)", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/home-gym/">Home Gym</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Buying Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Best Weight Plates for a Home Gym: Iron, Rubber and Urethane</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        Weight plates are the simplest thing in a home gym and the easiest to overpay for. The real differences are the coating, the hole size, how close each plate is to its stated weight, whether it has handles, and what it costs per pound once you add up a full set. Here are five currently sold options from about $1.25 to about $3.45 per pound, and how to choose.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated October 19, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#top-picks" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <figure class="ed-hero">
        <div class="ed-hero-collage">
          <a href="#rep-equalizer-urethane-plates"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41fk1d-ZFNL._SL500_.jpg" alt="REP Urethane Coated Equalizer Plates (45 lb Pair)" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#titan-cast-iron-olympic-plate"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/410NIbgcZvL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Titan Fitness 45 LB Cast Iron Olympic Plate" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#rep-rubber-coated-olympic-plates"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41knkiYW47L._SL500_.jpg" alt="REP Rubber Coated Olympic Plates (45 lb Pair)" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
        </div>
        <figcaption>REP Urethane Coated Equalizer Plates (45 lb Pair) · Titan Fitness 45 LB Cast Iron Olympic Plate · REP Rubber Coated Olympic Plates (45 lb Pair)</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          The best weight plates for most home gyms are Olympic 2-inch plates with grip handles,
          bought as a set that matches the lifts you do, from a maker that publishes a weight
          tolerance. Plain cast iron is the cheapest per pound and lasts for decades; rubber or
          urethane coatings cost more and make plates quieter and kinder to floors and bars, but
          none of them makes iron plates safe to drop. If you will drop the bar, you want bumper
          plates instead. The five picks below cover cast iron, rubber-coated and urethane-coated plates, and
          the guide after them explains tolerance, hole sizes and how to price a set.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
          <h2>Our top picks:</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Overall:</span> <a href="#rep-equalizer-urethane-plates">REP Urethane Coated Equalizer Plates (45 lb Pair)</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Value on Amazon:</span> <a href="#titan-cast-iron-olympic-plate">Titan Fitness 45 LB Cast Iron Olympic Plate</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[1].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Coated Iron on Amazon:</span> <a href="#rep-rubber-coated-olympic-plates">REP Rubber Coated Olympic Plates (45 lb Pair)</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[2].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Lowest Price:</span> <a href="#cap-olympic-grip-plates">CAP Barbell Olympic Cast Iron Grip Plates</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[3].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Rubber-Coated on Amazon:</span> <a href="#titan-grip-plates-pair">Titan Fitness Olympic Grip Weight Plates (5 lb Pair)</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[4].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the products below.</strong> We have not tested these
          plates, and no figure on this page is our own measurement. On 19 October 2026 we confirmed
          each plate as currently listed on its maker's own store (REP Fitness, CAP Barbell and Titan
          Fitness) and checked its weights, hole size, tolerance and warranty against at least one
          independent source: Garage Gym Reviews' weight plate guide, its in-depth review of REP's
          Equalizer iron plates and its CAP grip plate page, and BarBend's weight plate guide. A
          figure appears on a card only where the maker and an independent source agree; where they
          conflicted — on one plate's warranty and finish — we left it out and say so on the card.
          Titan's grip plate set is the exception: we found no independent review of it, so its card
          carries Titan's own figures only and says so. <strong class="text-gray-900">Where to buy.</strong> Amazon's pages would not load for us
          that day, so Amazon availability was confirmed from Amazon US listings returned by search
          and from review sites' Amazon links: we found CAP's grip
          plates listed there. On 9 November 2026 we found REP's urethane Equalizer plates on Amazon,
          listed by REP as its Urethane Coated Plate v3, and linked the card to them; we could not find
          REP's US-made Equalizer iron plates on Amazon, so we replaced that card with REP's
          rubber-coated Olympic plates, checked against REP's page and FAQ, its Amazon listings and a
          Newegg listing. The Equalizer iron plates are still discussed in the guide below. REP's Old
          School plates and Titan's 245 lb grip set are sold on their makers' stores and could not be
          matched to Amazon US listings, so on 9 November 2026 their cards became Titan's cast-iron
          Olympic plate and Titan's 5 lb grip-plate pair, the closest plates Amazon sells, carded on
          Titan's own figures and Amazon's listings that day.
          Every brand was checked against the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database
          on the same date, along with weight plates as a category. Single plates and small pairs
          cost well under $100; the price ranges on the cards cover pairs and packs, and per-pound
          figures are our arithmetic from the prices we saw. Prices change often. We earn a
          commission if you buy through the Amazon links on this page; it does not change which
          products we list or what we say about them. <strong class="text-gray-900">What this page is, and is not.</strong> We review home
