          <p>
            Two patterns are worth knowing before you order. Small plates cost far more per pound than
            big ones: a pair of REP Old School 2.5s is $22.50, or $4.50 a pound, against $1.89 for the
            45s. That is why a set heavy in 45s looks cheap per pound, and why the CAP 285 lb pack beats
            CAP's own singles. And makers' own set prices may differ from the sum of the pairs — REP
            lists six set sizes for each plate, from 175 to 425 lb — so price the set and the pairs
            before you choose.
          </p>
          <p>
            Shipping is the hidden cost. Plates are heavy, and a "cheap" set with a large freight
            charge can cost more than a dearer one shipped free. Check the delivered price, and whether
            a local store can sell you the same plates without freight.
