        <div id="how-much" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Much Weight Should You Buy?</h2>
<p>
            Buy for the heaviest lift you will do in the next year, usually the deadlift, plus the
            small plates you need to progress. A common starting point is a 245 lb set — a pair each of
            45, 35, 25, 10, 5 and 2.5 lb plates — which with a 45 lb bar loads up to 290 lb. One pair
            of each leaves a few gaps (you cannot make 85 lb, for example); add a second pair of 10s
            and every 5 lb step from 50 to 310 lb is covered. A 175 lb set without the 35s is enough for many beginners for a few
            months, and a second pair of 45s is the usual first addition.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you lift with a partner of very different strength, extra small plates matter more than
            extra 45s, because you will be changing the bar constantly. If you also use dumbbell
            handles or a plate-loaded machine, count the plates those need at the same time as the bar.
          </p>
          <p>
            Store plates on a tree or on your rack's plate horns, low and evenly loaded, not stacked
            on the floor or leaning against a wall. Our <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">squat rack guide</a> explains why heavy storage belongs low on the rack, our <a href="/best-weight-benches/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight bench guide</a> covers the bench you will press from, and our <a href="/best-home-gym-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym accessories guide</a> covers plate trees and collars.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, protect the floor. Iron plates set down on concrete chip both the plates and the
            slab; on wood, tile or vinyl they dent and crack. Even if you never drop the bar, a layer of <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">rubber gym flooring</a> under the lifting area is worth having, and a <a href="/best-lifting-platforms/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifting platform</a> is the answer for heavy deadlifts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying Weight Plates</h2>
          <p>Each of these is easy to avoid before you order and expensive to correct afterwards.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying standard plates for an Olympic bar</h3>
              <p>Cheap 1-inch standard plates will not fit a 2-inch Olympic sleeve properly, and adapters are a poor fix. Check the centre hole — about 2 inches for Olympic plates — before ordering, especially when buying used or in a bundle.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Treating coated plates as bumpers</h3>
              <p>Rubber and urethane coatings protect against knocks, not drops. Dropping coated iron plates can crack them, bend the bar and damage the floor. If your training includes drops, buy bumper plates for those lifts.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Comparing prices by the plate, not the pound</h3>
              <p>A set full of small plates looks cheap but costs more per pound, and freight can turn the cheapest set into the dearest. Work out the delivered price per pound for the plates you actually need before you compare.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Skipping collars and uneven loading</h3>
              <p>Plates slide on a moving bar, and one side loaded more than the other can tip a bar off its J-cups. Load and unload both sides alternately, use collars on every set, and keep heavy plates low on the rack's storage.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For most home gyms, buy REP's Old School iron plates</strong> if
            budget comes first, at about $2.16 a pound for a full set, or REP's urethane-coated
            Equalizers if you want quiet, odour-free plates with handles and can pay about $3.45. CAP's
            grip plates are the cheapest reputable option on Amazon, Titan's cast-iron plates are the
            Amazon match for REP's Old School plates, REP's rubber-coated plates add handles and stainless collars for less than urethane, and
            Titan's rubber-coated grip plates sit in between. For US manufacture, REP's Equalizer iron
            plates are sold on REP's own store.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever you buy, get Olympic 2-inch plates, weigh and pair them when they arrive, use
            collars, and never drop iron plates, coated or not. If dropping is part of your training, <a href="/best-bumper-plates/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">our bumper plate guide</a> covers the plates built for it.
          </p>
        </div>

